# Striatum: Supervised Sessions

RFC 0009 holds a single agent CLI alive across multiple work
packets. Packets arrive as newline-terminated JSON on stdin
(delivered by `striatum supervise send`); the supervised process
reads them line-by-line and reacts through the normal CLI verbs.

## When to invoke

- The lane has `harness_profile.supervision.compatible: true` and
  the operator wants the agent CLI held across packets.
- The operator mentioned `supervise start` / `supervise send`.

## Authoritative reference

{verbs_supervise}

Run `striatum <verb> --help` for full flag sets.

## Common patterns

```bash
# Register a fresh session for a supervised lane.
SESSION=$(striatum --repo $REPO register-session --run-id $RUN \
    --role $ROLE --lane $LANE --fresh --json | jq -r .data.session_id)

# Start the long-lived supervised process.
striatum --repo $REPO supervise start --session-id $SESSION --json

# Claim work and use the top-level packet id from claim-next output.
CLAIM=$(striatum --repo $REPO claim-next --session-id $SESSION --json)
PACKET_ID=$(printf '%s\n' "$CLAIM" | jq -r .data.packet_id)

# Equivalent copy-paste command is also available at:
#   jq -r .data.next_steps.supervise_send
striatum --repo $REPO supervise send \
    --session-id $SESSION --packet-id $PACKET_ID --json

striatum --repo $REPO supervise stop \
    --session-id $SESSION --reason operator_requested --json
```

The supervised process reads packets from stdin one line at a
time. Stdout and stderr are sent to `DEVNULL`; the runner does not
parse supervisor output for state.

Use `data.packet_id` from `claim-next`; `message_id`, `lease_id`,
`job_id`, `session_id`, and `supervisor_id` are different identifiers
and are not accepted as `--packet-id`.

